00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:10.350
KATHERINE MILLER: Hi, Aja.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:14.296
I hope you're having a good last
day of Google Cloud Next 2018.

00:00:14.296 --> 00:00:16.129
AJA HAMMERLY: It's been
fantastic this year.

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:17.137
The event is so huge.

00:00:17.137 --> 00:00:18.220
KATHERINE MILLER: Awesome.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.090
I am very excited to learn
more about disruption detector

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:22.827
from you today.

00:00:22.827 --> 00:00:25.410
AJA HAMMERLY: So this is a demo
I came up with in December-ish

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:26.560
of this year.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.680
And the goal was to take people
and let them break things,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.220
and then see, in real time,
how our monitoring and logging

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.170
and error reporting
systems showed that

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.760
so that they had a
better idea of what

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.370
it would look like if they used
it for their own applications.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.841
Because we do all these
demos, but the whole goal

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:43.840
of most of the demos is
that everything stays up

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:44.689
all the time.

00:00:44.689 --> 00:00:46.480
So you don't get to
see what those bad days

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:47.570
on the internet look like.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.014
And so I wanted to give folks
the opportunity to do that.

00:00:50.014 --> 00:00:51.430
So what we've got
going on here is

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:52.600
we've actually got a whole
bunch of different ways

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:53.320
you can break stuff.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.778
The panel behind you has a
bunch of different kinds of HTTP

00:00:55.778 --> 00:00:57.184
errors that you can send.

00:00:57.184 --> 00:00:58.600
The panel behind
me sends traffic.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.975
And further down, we have
panels that kill load balancers

00:01:00.975 --> 00:01:02.050
and kill VMs.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.784
And all of that is acting
against a Kubernetes

00:01:04.784 --> 00:01:06.700
application that I'm
running in the background

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.860
that we're trying to
keep up, while everyone

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.170
at the conference here
tries to take it down.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.100
It's been fantastic.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.080
And then, once you're
done with all of that,

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.360
you get to see, up at the
top, how those things look

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:17.704
in Stackdriver in real time.

00:01:17.704 --> 00:01:18.870
KATHERINE MILLER: Excellent.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.120
And I know you mentioned
a number of products

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.654
on Google Cloud Platform,
as well as Kubernetes.

00:01:23.654 --> 00:01:25.570
Can you talk a little
bit about the technology

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.559
that went into building
disruption detector?

00:01:27.559 --> 00:01:29.100
AJA HAMMERLY: So it
wasn't my intent,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.430
but we've actually
managed to use

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:32.530
every single one of Google
Cloud's compute options

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.860
in this product or in this demo.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.030
We have Cloud functions backing
both the errors and the load.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:41.520
We have Kubernetes, obviously,
is the app that we're running.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.220
I am using Compute Engine to run
a cron job that puts everything

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.780
back together after
the attendees break it.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.271
And I'm using App
Engine as a test

00:01:49.271 --> 00:01:51.270
harness so that I could
build this independently

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.970
of the manufacturing
of the demo.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.190
And that's also going
to potentially let

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.570
us take this demo
to events where

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:56.980
we don't have this much space.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.521
And we can do it as
a web-based demo.

00:01:58.521 --> 00:02:00.820
KATHERINE MILLER:
And what makes this

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.869
interesting and useful and
helpful to our developers?

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:06.160
AJA HAMMERLY: So as much as
we don't want it to happen,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.343
stuff always goes wrong.

00:02:07.343 --> 00:02:08.759
I was talking
yesterday in my talk

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:11.110
that, even if you write
the best code ever

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.824
and your infrastructure
is perfect,

00:02:12.824 --> 00:02:14.740
you could always have a
day when a shark bites

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.270
through the undersea
fiber cable,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.190
because that's
actually happened.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.900
And so what this
teaches you and teaches

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.570
developers is that if they
use Google Cloud's Stackdriver

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.027
offering, we have a bunch
of different products

00:02:25.027 --> 00:02:26.860
that all play well
together and can give you

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.470
an overview of your system,
so you can quickly figure out

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.780
what's wrong and then fix it.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.795
And it's really, really
interesting for folks,

00:02:33.795 --> 00:02:35.170
because, in the
past, people have

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.836
been putting these
technologies together

00:02:36.836 --> 00:02:38.440
from a variety of
different vendors.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.320
But if they use Stackdriver
on GCP, they all come.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:43.090
We have all the
options already here.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:43.990
KATHERINE MILLER: Excellent.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:45.370
Well, thank you very
much for the tour.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:46.990
And I hope you enjoy the
last day of the conference.

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:47.906
AJA HAMMERLY: You too.

